Today, I learned how to work with WordPress child themes, which allow me to customize a website without modifying the parent theme directly. Here’s a summary of what I covered:

    1.Creating a Child Theme:
        I created a child theme folder and added a style.css and functions.php file.
        I learned how to properly enqueue both parent and child theme styles using the wp_enqueue_style() function in functions.php.

    2.Customizing CSS:
        I modified the CSS in the child theme to change the appearance of the site, such as changing the color of the site title and adding custom styling for other elements.

    3.Debugging:
        I explored how to enable debugging in WordPress using the wp-config.php file to check if my customizations were loaded correctly. I also looked for the debug.log file in the wp-content directory to troubleshoot any issues.

    4.Adding Footer Customization:
        I learned how to add a custom footer in the child theme by modifying the footer.php file or using CSS to change the footer’s appearance.

    5.Activating and Testing the Child Theme:
        I activated the child theme in the WordPress admin dashboard and tested the changes by inspecting the source code to ensure that the child theme’s CSS was being applied correctly.

    6.Exploring Further Customizations:
        I explored various other customizations such as modifying fonts, colors, the navigation menu, and adding custom JavaScript. I also learned how to create custom page templates and functions in the child theme.

Overall, this session was about understanding how to safely extend and customize WordPress using child themes. This allows me to make updates and changes to a site without interfering with the core files of the parent theme, ensuring that updates to the parent theme don’t overwrite my customizations.


